{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central thesis is that within a single evaluation one 'cannot definitively distinguish' genuine reliability gain from mere compliance tracking, because single-evaluation evidence is structurally insufficient. Yet SRC_001's own counterconditions list exactly the within-single-evidence designs that accomplish this: (a) a single evaluation can include 'adversarial items specifically designed to break surface correlations,' and SRC_001 concedes this 'could suffice within one evaluation'; and (b) if 'the checklist is provably causally complete (deductive),' the distinction collapses within that evaluation. The problem explicitly asks to '[i]dentify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish' the two, not to argue the distinction is impossible. SRC_001 is therefore self-refuting: it denies the possibility while listing feasible within-evaluation mechanisms that satisfy the problem's demand.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Adversarial probes in one evaluation break checklist-vs-ground-truth surface correlations.","result":"The checklist fails while the expert's intuition holds, demonstrating that the prior reliability was mere surface-compliance tracking — a distinction made entirely within the single evaluation."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's answer assumes the non-expert panel's reliability advantage comes from faithful execution of the checklist author's 'frozen-good-theory,' explicitly arguing the value is not aggregation/Condorcet. But the problem asks whether a panel of trained non-experts following an explicit checklist will, on average, be more reliable than a single expert. SRC_002 gives no argument or evidence that panel aggregation is not the operative reliability mechanism: Condorcet-style error reduction and redundancy could yield higher reliability than a single expert even if the checklist is only a surface proxy with no superior causal structure. By attributing reliability solely to checklist-theory quality, SRC_002 fails to argue the actual comparative claim (panel-vs-single) posed by the problem.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Checklist items are shallow surface heuristics, but a large panel of non-experts independently following them reduces random execution errors, while a single expert's intuition is subject to correlated biases.","result":"Panel mean outperforms single expert even though the checklist encodes no superior causal theory."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}